  "statement": "Given an array `nums` of **distinct** positive integers, return _the number of tuples_ `(a, b, c, d)` _such that_ `a * b = c * d` _where_ `a`_,_ `b`_,_ `c`_, and_ `d` _are elements of_ `nums`_, and_ `a != b != c != d`_._\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [2,3,4,6]\nOutput: 8\nExplanation: There are 8 valid tuples:\n(2,6,3,4) , (2,6,4,3) , (6,2,3,4) , (6,2,4,3)\n(3,4,2,6) , (4,3,2,6) , (3,4,6,2) , (4,3,6,2)\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [1,2,4,5,10]\nOutput: 16\nExplanation: There are 16 valid tuples:\n(1,10,2,5) , (1,10,5,2) , (10,1,2,5) , (10,1,5,2)\n(2,5,1,10) , (2,5,10,1) , (5,2,1,10) , (5,2,10,1)\n(2,10,4,5) , (2,10,5,4) , (10,2,4,5) , (10,2,5,4)\n(4,5,2,10) , (4,5,10,2) , (5,4,2,10) , (5,4,10,2)\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `1 <= nums.length <= 1000`\n*   `1 <= nums[i] <= 10^(4)`\n*   All elements in `nums` are **distinct**.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= nums.length <= 1000",
    "1 <= nums[i] <= 104",
    "All elements in nums are distinct."
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
